@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "How It Works";
    ViewData["OnboardingPage"] = true;
}

<div class="learn12wr-page">

    <section class="learn12wr-hero">
        <div class="learn12wr-kicker">WELCOME TO 12 WEEK RECOMP</div>

        <h1>
            This is a learning program,
            <strong>not just a tracking app.</strong>
        </h1>

        <p class="learn12wr-hero-lead">
            For the next 12 weeks, your job is to move, fuel, measure,
            log, and learn. The more consistently you follow the process,
            the more useful the system becomes - and the more you learn
            about how your own body actually responds.
        </p>

        <div class="learn12wr-key">
            <strong>The key is consistency, not perfection.</strong>
            <span>
                Be dedicated to following the program for as many of the
                12 weeks as you can. One imperfect day does not matter.
                Repeated days of useful activity, honest data, and attention
                to your results are what create the learning.
            </span>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="learn12wr-section learn12wr-visual-section">
        <div class="learn12wr-section-number">1</div>

        <div class="learn12wr-section-copy">
            <div class="learn12wr-kicker">THE DRIVER OF THE PROGRAM</div>
            <h2>Move your body. Resistance training matters most.</h2>

            <p>
                Activity is what gives 12 Week Recomp something to work with.
                Resistance training is especially important because lifting
                weights gives your body the signal to build or preserve muscle,
                improve strength, and develop a firmer, more defined physique.
            </p>

            <p>
                That does not mean every day should be a hard lifting day.
                Walking, Zone 2 cardio, conditioning, mobility work, sports,
                and recovery movement all have a place.
            </p>

            <div class="learn12wr-callout">
                <strong>Do something purposeful with your body every day.</strong>
                Hard days should create a useful training signal. Easier days
                should help you recover while still keeping you active.
            </div>
        </div>

        <img src="~/images/onboarding-v1009/resistance-training.svg"
             asp-append-version="true"
             class="learn12wr-illustration"
             alt="Person performing resistance training with a barbell" />
    </section>

    <section class="learn12wr-section">
        <div class="learn12wr-section-number">2</div>
        <div>
            <div class="learn12wr-kicker">YOUR INDIVIDUALIZED ENGINE</div>
            <h2>Your activity helps determine your food prescription.</h2>

            <p>
                The individualized engine powering 12WR looks at the activity
                you plan, the activity you actually complete, your body
                measurements, nutrition, recovery information, and the other
                data you provide.
            </p>

            <p>
                It then develops a daily calorie and macronutrient prescription
                for you.
            </p>

            <div class="learn12wr-fuel-rule">
                <strong>The harder the activity you have planned, the more fuel you may need.</strong>
                <span>
                    That is intentional. Hard resistance and conditioning work
                    can require more carbohydrate to support performance and
                    restore glycogen. Easier or recovery days may require less.
                </span>
            </div>

            <p>
                <strong>Use the fuel the program gives you.</strong>
                More food on a demanding training day is not automatically
                working against fat loss. Proper fueling can help you train
                harder, recover better, preserve muscle, and continue making
                progress.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="learn12wr-section">
        <div class="learn12wr-section-number">3</div>
        <div>
            <div class="learn12wr-kicker">CHOOSE YOUR DIRECTION</div>
            <h2>Tell 12WR what you want to emphasize.</h2>

            <p>
                During setup, choose the recomp approach that best matches your
                primary goal: fat-loss focused, muscle-gain focused, or a more
                balanced approach.
            </p>

            <div class="learn12wr-goal-grid">
                <article>
                    <strong>Fat-loss focused</strong>
                    <p>
                        Emphasizes reducing body fat while protecting muscle
                        and supporting useful training.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article>
                    <strong>Balanced recomp</strong>
                    <p>
                        Gives similar priority to improving body composition,
                        training performance, and recovery.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article>
                    <strong>Muscle-gain focused</strong>
                    <p>
                        Emphasizes building muscle and strength while limiting
                        unnecessary fat gain.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="learn12wr-recomp-emphasis">
                <strong>We are not just tracking scale weight.</strong>
                <p>
                    12 Week Recomp is trying to understand two things at the
                    same time: <strong>fat loss and muscle gain or preservation.</strong>
                    A lower scale number is not automatically better if muscle
                    is being lost, and a flat or slightly higher scale number
                    can still be excellent progress when body fat is falling
                    while lean mass improves.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="learn12wr-section">
        <div class="learn12wr-section-number">4</div>
        <div>
            <div class="learn12wr-kicker">PLAN THE WEEK</div>
            <h2>Set up the activities you realistically expect to do.</h2>

            <p>
                On the <strong>Weekly Plan</strong> page, enter the activities
                you expect to perform for the coming week. Use the guidance on
                that page to build a week you can realistically complete.
            </p>

            <p>
                Your plan gives the engine advance notice of the training demand
                coming up, which helps it prepare the appropriate nutrition
                prescription.
            </p>

            <div class="learn12wr-link-row">
                <a asp-controller="Schedule"
                   asp-action="Index"
                   class="learn12wr-secondary-button">
                    Open Weekly Plan
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="learn12wr-daily">
        <div class="learn12wr-kicker">YOUR DAILY ROUTINE</div>
        <h2>A simple cycle, repeated consistently.</h2>

        <div class="learn12wr-daily-grid">

            <article class="learn12wr-daily-card learn12wr-daily-card-visual">
                <div>
                    <div class="learn12wr-daily-step">MORNING</div>
                    <h3>1. Measure more than weight when you can</h3>

                    <p>
                        Weigh yourself under reasonably consistent morning
                        conditions and enter your daily scale readings.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        <strong>12WR is not just tracking body weight.</strong>
                        We are looking for the relationship between
                        <strong>fat loss and muscle gain or preservation.</strong>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        If your scale only gives body weight, use it - the system
                        will still work with the information available. But you
                        are giving the engine less evidence, so body-composition
                        analysis and individualized adjustments are less precise.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        A body-composition/BIA scale that estimates body fat and
                        muscle or lean mass gives 12WR much more useful trend
                        information. These scales are relatively inexpensive and
                        widely available online. If you do not have one now,
                        consider picking up an inexpensive compatible
                        body-composition scale at some point during the program.
                    </p>

                    <p class="learn12wr-note">
                        Consumer BIA readings can move with hydration, glycogen,
                        sodium, and other short-term factors. We use them as
                        repeated trend evidence - not as a literal one-day
                        measurement of fat or muscle tissue.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <img src="~/images/onboarding-v1009/body-composition-scale.svg"
                     asp-append-version="true"
                     class="learn12wr-card-illustration"
                     alt="Body composition scale showing weight, body fat and muscle measurements" />
            </article>

            <article>
                <div class="learn12wr-daily-step">DURING THE DAY</div>
                <h3>2. Move</h3>
                <p>
                    Complete the activity you planned. If life changes and you
                    do something different, do the activity that makes sense
                    and tell 12WR what actually happened.
                </p>
            </article>

            <article>
                <div class="learn12wr-daily-step">AFTER ACTIVITY</div>
                <h3>3. Log the workout</h3>
                <p>
                    Go to <strong>Daily Entry</strong> and enter the workout or
                    activity you actually completed, including duration and
                    effort when available.
                </p>
            </article>

            <article class="learn12wr-daily-card learn12wr-daily-card-visual">
                <div>
                    <div class="learn12wr-daily-step">THROUGHOUT THE DAY</div>
                    <h3>4. Track your food</h3>
                    <p>
                        Use the nutrition app of your choice to track what you
                        eat. Many useful options are available for free.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        At the end of the day - or the next morning - enter your
                        daily calories, protein, carbohydrate, and fat totals
                        into 12WR.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <img src="~/images/onboarding-v1009/food-tracking-app.svg"
                     asp-append-version="true"
                     class="learn12wr-card-illustration"
                     alt="Phone displaying daily calorie, protein, carbohydrate and fat tracking" />
            </article>

            <article>
                <div class="learn12wr-daily-step">NEXT MORNING</div>
                <h3>5. Learn from the Dashboard</h3>
                <p>
                    12WR compares what was planned, what actually happened,
                    what you ate, and how your body responded. The Dashboard
                    explains the result and gives you the next prescription.
                </p>
            </article>

            <article>
                <div class="learn12wr-daily-step">REPEAT</div>
                <h3>6. Adapt</h3>
                <p>
                    Keep following the cycle. One day is information. Repeated
                    days create evidence. Trends are where the useful learning
                    happens.
                </p>
            </article>

        </div>

        <div class="learn12wr-cycle">
            <span>Measure</span>
            <b>&rarr;</b>
            <span>Move</span>
            <b>&rarr;</b>
            <span>Log</span>
            <b>&rarr;</b>
            <span>Fuel</span>
            <b>&rarr;</b>
            <span>Learn</span>
            <b>&rarr;</b>
            <span>Adapt</span>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="learn12wr-section">
        <div class="learn12wr-section-number">5</div>
        <div>
            <div class="learn12wr-kicker">YOU DO NOT HAVE TO BE PERFECT</div>
            <h2>Give the engine the best information you reasonably can.</h2>

            <p>
                You do not have to enter every possible data point every day.
                More complete and accurate information gives 12WR more evidence,
                but the engine is designed to make the best prescription and
                analysis it can from the information that is available.
            </p>

            <div class="learn12wr-example-grid">
                <div>
                    <strong>Only have body weight?</strong>
                    <span>Enter it. It is still useful.</span>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <strong>Have body fat and muscle readings?</strong>
                    <span>Enter them too. They strengthen recomp trend analysis.</span>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <strong>Workout changed?</strong>
                    <span>Log what you actually did.</span>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <strong>Only know calories and protein?</strong>
                    <span>Enter what you know.</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p>
                Honest imperfect data is more useful than a perfect-looking
                entry that does not reflect what really happened.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="learn12wr-finish">
        <div class="learn12wr-kicker">WHAT THE 12 WEEKS ARE REALLY FOR</div>

        <h2>Stay with the process long enough to learn your own body.</h2>

        <p>
            The real key is to be consistent and dedicated to the program for
            as many of the 12 weeks as you can. This is a learning tool, not
            just a tracking app.
        </p>

        <p>
            After you have diligently collected a meaningful portion of the
            12 weeks, completed your activity, paid attention to your nutrition,
            read the coaching messages, and watched your results, you should
            have a much clearer understanding of what your body needs and which
            habits actually work for you.
        </p>

        <p>
            The goal is to leave you with repeatable activity and eating habits
            that can help you work toward the body you want while supporting
            long-term strength, health, and quality of life.
        </p>

        <div class="learn12wr-graduate">
            <strong>And when the 12 weeks are over?</strong>

            <p>
                You are welcome to continue with 12 Week Recomp for another
                term and keep refining the process.
            </p>

            <p>
                Or you can let the app go.
            </p>

            <p>
                If you finish with the knowledge, habits, and confidence to
                make good decisions without us, we will be thrilled. That means
                the program did one of the most important things it was built
                to do: teach you the information many people wish they had
                learned years ago about how training, food, recovery, and their
                own body actually work together.
            </p>
        </div>

        <p class="learn12wr-results-note">
            Individual results vary. Consistency, training quality, nutrition,
            recovery, health status, and starting point all affect the rate and
            magnitude of change.
        </p>

        <div class="learn12wr-actions">
            <a asp-controller="Intake"
               asp-action="Index"
               class="learn12wr-primary-button">
                Start My 12 Weeks
            </a>

            <a asp-controller="Home"
               asp-action="Evidence"
               class="learn12wr-secondary-button">
                Read the Evidence
            </a>
        </div>
    </section>

</div>